<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Soomra dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1026 CE – 1351 CE (approx.) · capital Thari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A local dynasty of Sindh that ruled the lower Indus for three centuries between the Ghaznavid conquest and the Sammas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Soomras were a Sindhi clan of debated origin, perhaps local converts linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> community. They rose to power in the 11th century as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid</a> control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> weakened after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habbari-dynasty">Habbari dynasty</a>, establishing their seat at Thari and later at other towns of lower Sindh.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known in detail, as the sources are late Persian histories and Sindhi folk tradition. The Soomras appear to have had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> sympathies and to have tolerated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> customs. Sindhi folk poetry celebrates figures of the period, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodo-soomro">Dodo Soomro</a>, who resisted the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, and Umar Soomro, the king of the tale of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-marvi">Umar and Marvi</a>. Agriculture on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> and trade through its ports sustained the realm.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Soomras faced repeated pressure from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. In the mid-14th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samma-dynasty">Samma dynasty</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a>, displaced them, and the Soomras became local chiefs.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1026 CE – 1351 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.12%;width:6.47%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1026 CE – 1351 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dodo Soomro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ismaili Shia Islam, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sindhi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Soomras, Sumra dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1188&amp;focus=soomra-dynasty" aria-label="Soomra dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6456 1104 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Soomra dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M6803 1523h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6776 1360h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6776 1360h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Pakistan. <a href="/atlas?year=1188&amp;focus=soomra-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
